{"ts": "2026-10-26T21:59:46.668820Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2159Z-7973", "unit": "openspec:add-tenancy-foundation", "seq": 6, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-001", "target": "F-9d433ef5", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding is waived. The design already decides this case: a failed reset destroys the connection, and when the connection dies the connection is destroyed and the original error is rethrown. The code does exactly that: a reset error after a successful COMMIT marks the client for destruction, is kept as the failure when nothing earlier failed, and is rethrown after release. The reset is a plain set_config of a session-level custom setting, which fails only when the connection itself is broken, so this is the design's \"connection dies\" row, not a case the documents leave open. A caller that sees a connection error after sending COMMIT can never be sure whether the writes landed, which is the general post-commit ambiguity of any database client, and the judge's own suggested behaviour restates what the code does now.", "text_sha256": "7be597b09fff45c8247b83f9156016b7749f0429d5c70013f17d101bb8cb80df", "answer_sha256": "d5c85c5d8973e4a9a67258d01c4e6ed15149d8b3f4edd56271e5d407e17db613", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
